{
 "turns_text": [
  "[SEG0001] person_1 (NURSE): alright, room three is yours. blood pressure is one twenty-eight over seventy-six, heart rate seventy-two, temperature ninety-eight even, and your oxygen is ninety-eight percent. the doctor will be in shortly.",
  "[SEG0002] person_2 (PATIENT): thanks, umm, sorry, I was chatting the receptionist's ear off out there, I do that when I'm jumpy. the reason I came in is a mole on my back.",
  "[SEG0003] person_3 (DOCTOR): you're fine. start wherever it makes sense, and I'll jump in.",
  "[SEG0004] person_2 (PATIENT): so it's on my upper back, toward the left shoulder blade, and I've had it five years, uh, no, that's wrong, we bought the house eight years ago and it was already there, so eight years. it never hurt, I just noticed it when I toweled off, and I kind of filed it under normal body stuff. my partner is the one who got worried, not me.",
  "[SEG0005] person_4 (CAREGIVER): yeah I'm his partner, I live with him. the color has changed, it's darker, almost black along one edge, and that black was not there a few months ago.",
  "[SEG0006] person_2 (PATIENT): I can't see it properly, two mirrors just confuse me, so she took a phone photo and zoomed in. I mean I believe her, she's way more observant about this stuff than I am, she notices if I change my shoelaces.",
  "[SEG0007] person_3 (DOCTOR): so, eight years, left upper back, and the color has gotten darker over a few months. has it actually bled?",
  "[SEG0008] person_2 (PATIENT): once, yes. I stepped out of the shower, the towel had a thin streak of blood, and I was sure it was a scratch until I realized it lined up with the mole. I cannot pin the day, sometime in the last month, maybe three weeks, I really don't know.",
  "[SEG0009] person_4 (CAREGIVER): Mm. just the once. there was a small spot on his gray t-shirt too, not much blood, but it was from that mole.",
  "[SEG0010] person_3 (DOCTOR): one bleed, date fuzzy. does the mole itch, and has it gotten bigger?",
  "[SEG0011] person_2 (PATIENT): it itches off and on, a few times a week, and I sit on my hands so I don't scratch it, which is harder than it sounds when you're at your desk all day. it has not gotten bigger, the spring photo and the one from this week look the same size to me, it's just the color that's off.",
  "[SEG0012] person_3 (DOCTOR): any fevers?",
  "[SEG0013] person_2 (PATIENT): no, no fevers. I've felt pretty much like my usual self that way.",
  "[SEG0014] person_3 (DOCTOR): any cough?",
  "[SEG0015] person_2 (PATIENT): no cough. not even a tickle, and I would notice because I talk all day at work.",
  "[SEG0016] person_3 (DOCTOR): have you lost any weight?",
  "[SEG0017] person_2 (PATIENT): err, I don't really weigh myself, so I can't say. my jeans might be a little looser, but I've also been skipping breakfast, so I honestly don't know if that's a real change or just the breakfast thing.",
  "[SEG0018] person_3 (DOCTOR): alright, no fever, no cough, and the weight is too muddy to call. when you were younger, did you get much sun, any bad burns?",
  "[SEG0019] person_2 (PATIENT): oh, a lot. I grew up in Arizona, little league and then the pool all summer, and I got blistering sunburns more than once as a teenager, the peeling kind, sheets stuck to my back and everything. we basically never used sunscreen, which I know sounds ridiculous now.",
  "[SEG0020] person_3 (DOCTOR): what medicines do you take, and do you have any allergies?",
  "[SEG0021] person_2 (PATIENT): umm, I take lisinipril by mouth for my blood pressure, ten milligrams, no, sorry, they increased it, it's twenty milligrams every morning. I also take vitamin D, two thousand units, once a day. and baby aspirin, eighty-one milligrams most mornings, I always say it's for my knees but actually my doctor put me on it for my heart. no drug allergies, penicillin is fine for me, my sister gets a rash but I don't.",
  "[SEG0022] person_3 (DOCTOR): so high blood pressure, lisinopril twenty milligrams each morning, vitamin D, aspirin for your heart, and no allergies. I'll take a look now, if you can sit forward.",
  "[SEG0023] person_2 (PATIENT): yeah the gown is open. there's an old scar lower down from falling off my bike as a kid, that's not the mole, the mole sits higher, I always point at the wrong one.",
  "[SEG0024] person_3 (DOCTOR): on the left upper back I see a mole about eight millimeters across. it is asymmetric, the border is irregular, and the color is uneven, brown with a black area at one edge. it is not bleeding today, the skin around it looks normal, and I don't feel any swollen nodes in your neck or left armpit.",
  "[SEG0025] person_2 (PATIENT): is the black edge the part that matters? I was up late reading about borders and colors and I kind of spiraled, which my partner can confirm, I had like six tabs open.",
  "[SEG0026] person_3 (DOCTOR): I think the right move is we photograph it today and I see you back in three months to compare. most moles like this we can safely watch.",
  "[SEG0027] person_4 (CAREGIVER): wait, before you lock that in, his dad had a melanoma, they removed it from his shoulder around age sixty. and that black edge was not in the April photo, it came on faster than I like, I don't want to wait three months.",
  "[SEG0028] person_3 (DOCTOR): that changes things for me. with the new color, the one bleed, and your father having melanoma, I'm not going to watch this for three months. I'm referring you to dermatology, and they may biopsy it if they think it needs it, because I can't rule out an atypical mole versus a melanoma. wear a hat and sunscreen, don't pick at it, and if it bleeds again before they see you, come back in.",
  "[SEG0029] person_2 (PATIENT): okay, yeah, I can do the hat, I already bought the sport sunscreen and then left it in the car, which is very me. I'll bring the April photo to the skin doctor so they can see the change. thank you for not just waving me off, I was worried I was being dramatic.",
  "[SEG0030] person_1 (NURSE): I'll send the dermatology referral out today, and their office should call you to set the visit. you're all set to check out up front."
 ],
